<https://aa.perseus.org/staging/o/ham:287257> a stg:Object ;
    stg:aatType <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300193015> ;
    stg:accessionmethod "Not Recorded" ;
    stg:catalogPage <https://www.harvardartmuseums.org/collections/object/287257> ;
    stg:century "5th century BCE" ;
    stg:classification "Vessels" ;
    stg:colorName "#191919",
        "#323232",
        "#4b4b4b",
        "#646464",
        "#7d7d7d",
        "#967d64",
        "#969696",
        "#afafaf",
        "#c8c8c8",
        "#e1e1e1" ;
    stg:commentary """This little jug or pitcher depicts a boy feeding, teasing, or training a dog with food. This miniature pitcher shape is called a “chous” in ancient Greek. Made in Athens, this vessel type was popular in the late 5th century BCE.
These little pitchers are connected to a spring festival in Athens called the Anthesteria, a festival of Dionysos that was especially connected to the new wine of the year and the arrival of spring. Some scholars have suggested that these little pitchers were used in festival ritual for the first taste of wine for three-year old children. They are often decorated, appropriately, with child-related themes such as this.
The little dog is a type known in antiquity as a Melitan (Maltese) dog. It is commonly depicted as a pet in ancient Greek art (see the grave stele of Melisto, 1961.86, for another depiction of a child teasing a Melitan dog in the collection).""" ;
    stg:creditline "Harvard Art Museums/Arthur M. Sackler Museum, Loan from Estate of Donald Upham and Mrs. R. U. Hunter" ;
    stg:cultureAAT <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300386130> ;
    stg:datebegin -425 ;
    stg:dated "c. 420 BCE" ;
    stg:dateend -415 ;
    stg:department "Department of Ancient and Byzantine Art & Numismatics" ;
    stg:description "Small trefoil-mouthed chous (juglet); decoration in Six's Technique (added paint and yellowish diluted gloss): child training a dog. Handle missing." ;
    stg:dimensions "5.8 × 5.5 cm (2 5/16 × 2 3/16 in.)" ;
    stg:division "Asian and Mediterranean Art" ;
    stg:hasColor <https://aa.perseus.org/staging/color/287257/#191919>,
